{"records":[{"id":"6wyzgw8z8k","title":"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2018.04.046","description":"Original data used for plotting the figures belonging to the study \"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-22T12:41:39.154Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6wyzgw8z8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6wyzgw8z8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sven","last_name":"Rottenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Barazas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Repair","Olaparib","BRCA Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zxzgp65bx5","title":" PJ34 protects hair cells from cisplatin‐induced ototoxicity in vitro: possible relation to inhibition of PARP-1-AIF-mediated parthanatos","description":"PARP inhibitor PJ34 exerts anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective effects by regulating parthanatos. The objective of this study was to investigate the protective effects of PJ34 on auditory cells against cisplatin-induced injury as well as to explore the potential mechanisms involved in its action. Flow cytometry, immunofluorescence staining, Western blot, Mitochondrial membrane potential (MMP) assay and MitoSox red staining were employed to detect apoptosis, determine the expression levels of cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and AIF, measure MMP levels, and evaluate the levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the corresponding HEI-OC1 auditory cells, ovarian cancer cell lines, and mouse cochlear hair cells. Our results demonstrated that exposure to 30 μM cisplatin activated cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and induced AIF nuclear translocation, leading to a decrease in MMP and an increase in ROS levels, which induced auditory cell death. Treatment with 2.5 μM PJ34 attenuated the hyperactivation of PARP-1 and nuclear translocation of AIF after cisplatin exposure, rescued the reduction of MMP and ROS accumulation, and thus protected auditory cells from cisplatin-induced cellular damage. Furthermore, PJ34 enhanced the sensitivity of ovarian cancer cell lines to cisplatin treatment. In conclusion, our findings suggest that PJ34 may attenuate cisplatin-induced hair cell death by regulating PARP-1-mediated parthanatos. Interestingly, PJ34 shows promise as a potential novel therapeutic agent for the prevention and/or treatment of cisplatin-induced ototoxicity. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-09T13:49:17.994Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zxzgp65bx5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zxzgp65bx5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Huiming","last_name":"Nong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiru","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junhong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhixin","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82201293"}],"categories":["Otorhinolaryngology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wwbzvt3h3w","title":"PARP-1 and DDX21 in Breast Cancer Images","description":"These are the original microscopy images associated with Kim et al. \"Activation of PARP-1 by snoRNAs Controls Ribosome Biogenesis and Cell Growth via the RNA Helices DDX21,\" published in Molecular Cell in 2019.  These are IHC or IF images of DDX21, PARP-1, and related factors in MCF-7 cells, xenograft tumors from mice, and human breast cancer samples.  These images are from Figure 6A-B; Figure 7K, N, P; Supplemental Figure S6A, D; and Supplemental Figure S7F).\n\nSummary\nPARP inhibitors (PARPi) prevent cancer cell growth by inducing synthetic lethality with DNA repair defects (e.g., in BRCA1/2 mutant cells).  We have identified an alternate pathway for PARPi-mediated growth control in BRCA1/2-intact breast cancer cells involving rDNA transcription and ribosome biogenesis.  PARP-1 binds to snoRNAs, which stimulate PARP-1 catalytic activity in the nucleolus independent of DNA damage.  Activated PARP-1 ADP-ribosylates DDX21, an RNA helicase that localizes to nucleoli and promotes rDNA transcription when ADP-ribosylated.  Treatment with PARPi or mutation of the ADP-ribosylation sites reduce DDX21 nucleolar localization, rDNA transcription, ribosome biogenesis, protein translation, and cell growth.  The salient features of this pathway are evident in xenografts in mice and human breast cancer patient samples.  Elevated levels of PARP-1 and nucleolar DDX21 are associated with cancer-related outcomes.  Our studies provide a mechanistic rationale for efficacy of PARPi in cancer cells lacking defects in DNA repair whose growth is inhibited by PARPi.  ","source":{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-12T03:40:24.134Z","url":"https://elsevier.digitalcommonsdata.com/datasets/wwbzvt3h3w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wwbzvt3h3w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lee","last_name":"Kraus","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pntrtyfk7g","title":"DNA replication is highly resilient and persistent under the challenge of mild replication stress ","description":"Mitotic DNA synthesis (MiDAS) has been proposed to restart DNA synthesis during mitosis due to replication fork stalling in late interphase caused by mild replication stress (RS). Contrary to this proposal, we find that cells exposed to mild RS in fact maintain continued DNA replication throughout G2 and during G2-M transition in RAD51- and RAD52-dependent manners. Persistent DNA synthesis is necessary to resolve replication intermediates accumulated in G2 and disengage an ATR-imposed block to mitotic entry. Because of the continual nature, DNA synthesis at very late replication sites can overlap with chromosome condensation, generating the phenomenon of mitotic DNA synthesis. Unexpectedly, we find that the commonly-used CDK1 inhibitor, RO3306, interferes with replication to preclude detection of G2 DNA synthesis, leading to the impression of a mitosis-driven response. Our study reveals the importance of persistent DNA replication and checkpoint control to lessen the risk of severe genome under-replication under mild RS. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-04-14T14:37:00.767Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pntrtyfk7g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pntrtyfk7g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chris","last_name":"Chan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","Cell Cycle Control","Mitosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"96nzdf5s88","title":"Supp Material Differential immunomodulatory Effect of PARP Inhibition in BRCA1 deficient and competent tumor cells","description":"Supplementary Material to \"Differential immunomodulatory Effect of PARP Inhibition in BRCA1 deficient and competent tumor cells\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-14T10:19:36.093Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/96nzdf5s88","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/96nzdf5s88"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Isabel","last_name":"Alvarado-Cruz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariam ","last_name":"Mahmoud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohammed ","last_name":"Khan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shilin ","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sebastian ","last_name":"Oeck","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rithy ","last_name":"Meas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kaylyn ","last_name":"Clairmont","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Victoria ","last_name":"Quintana","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying ","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angelo ","last_name":"Porciuncula","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hailey ","last_name":"Wyatt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shuangge ","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yu ","last_name":"Shyr","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong ","last_name":"Kong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Patricia ","last_name":"LoRusso","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniel ","last_name":"Laverty","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zachary D. ","last_name":"Nagel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kurt A","last_name":"Schalper","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael ","last_name":"Krauthammer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joann B.","last_name":"Sweasy","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Poly ADP Ribose Polymerase","Immunotherapy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rrfg58k5h4","title":"Dataset of Transient HSP90 inhibition enhances sensitivity of mantle cell lymphoma to poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors","description":"The secondary data of Transient HSP90 inhibition enhances sensitivity of mantle cell lymphoma to poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-12-15T08:16:18.015Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rrfg58k5h4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rrfg58k5h4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chaowen","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Poly ADP Ribose Polymerase","B-Cell Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zbmchm3fz4","title":"Unrestrained poly-ADP-ribosylation provides insights into chromatin regulation and human disease Prokhorova et al.","description":"ARH3/ADPRHL2 and PARG are the primary enzymes reversing ADP-ribosylation in vertebrates, yet their functions in vivo remain unclear. ARH3 is the only hydrolase able to remove serine-linked mono(ADP-ribose) (MAR) but is much less efficient than PARG against poly(ADP-ribose) (PAR) chains in vitro. Here, by using ARH3-deficient cells, we demonstrate that endogenous MARylation persists on chromatin throughout the cell cycle, including mitosis, and is surprisingly well tolerated. Conversely, persistent PARylation is highly toxic and has distinct physiological effects, in particular on active transcription histone marks such as H3K9ac and H3K27ac. Furthermore, we reveal a synthetic lethal interaction between ARH3 and PARG and identify loss of ARH3 as a mechanism of PARP inhibitor resistance, both of which can be exploited in cancer therapy. Finally, we extend our findings to neurodegeneration, suggesting that patients with inherited ARH3 deficiency suffer from stress-induced pathogenic increase in PARylation that can be mitigated by PARP inhibition.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-10T20:34:28.357Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zbmchm3fz4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zbmchm3fz4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Ahel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Poly ADP Ribose Polymerase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"n9txt6y5cj","title":"TRABID deubiquitinase overexpression enables synthetic lethality to PARP inhibitor via prolonged retention of 53BP1 at DSB sites","description":"Here, we demonstrate that TRABID deubiquitinase (DUB) binds to 53BP1 at endogenous level and regulates 53BP1 retention at DSB sites. TRABID deubiquitinates K29-linked polyubiquitination of 53BP1 mediated by E3 ubiquitin ligase SPOP and prevents 53BP1 dissociation from DSBs, consequently inducing HR defects and chromosomal instability. Prostate cancer cells with TRABID overexpression exhibit a high sensitivity to poly (ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitors. This dataset include all the raw data of western blot from this project.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-27T11:33:09.418Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/n9txt6y5cj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/n9txt6y5cj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jian","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6c2w37v5g9","title":"Zombie Flow Cytometry","description":"Live Jurkat cells stained Caspase Violet, Zombie NIR, Cell ROX Green & MitoTracker Red. Fixed cells stained with RIP3_PE, Caspase-3-BV650, PARP-PE-CF594, H2AX-PE-Cy7","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-04T06:06:35.603Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6c2w37v5g9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6c2w37v5g9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Gary","last_name":"Warnes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Flow Cytometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8zsg9hhr6n","title":"EMC3 regulates mesenchymal cell survival via control of the mitotic spindle assembly","description":"Eukaryotic cells transit through the cell cycle to produce two daughter cells. Dysregulation of the cell cycle leads to cell death or tumorigenesis. Herein, we found that a subunit of the Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER) membrane complex, EMC3, is a key regulator of the cell cycle. Conditional deletion of Emc3 in mouse embryonic mesoderm led to reduced size and patterning defects of multiple organs. Emc3 deficiency impaired cell proliferation, causing spindle assembly defects, chromosome mis-segregation, cell cycle arrest at G2/M, and apoptosis. Upon entry into mitosis, mesenchymal cells upregulate EMC3 protein levels and localize EMC3 to the mitotic centrosomes. Further analysis indicated that EMC3 interacts with VCP and Aurora A in mitotic cells. EMC3 works together with VCP to tightly regulate the levels and activity of Aurora A, an essential factor for centrosome function and mitotic spindle assembly: while over-expression of EMC3 or VCP degraded Aurora A, loss of EMC3 or VCP led to increased stability but the reduced activity of Aurora A in mitosis. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-24T17:11:13.847Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8zsg9hhr6n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8zsg9hhr6n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jeffrey","last_name":"Whitsett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":133016,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":111,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":133016,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132286,"restricted":68,"embargoed":662},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2247},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":669},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":467},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":402},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":397},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":328},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":267},{"id":"https://ror.org/05r0vyz12","name":"Ministerio de Ciencia, Innovación y Universidades","count":224}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130388},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":186},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":131},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":56},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":27}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":510},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":407},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":356},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":355},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":303}]}}